 where A, B, C are row-major, with dimensions M, N, K. We'll use 2D thread blocks and shared memory tiling. For performance, we can use 16x16 or 32x32 tiles. We'll also handle bias addition optionally.\n\nSince we need multiple GEMMs with different sizes, we can call the same kernel with different parameters. For the small per-head GEMMs (batch_count = 1024, each 64x64x64), we could use a batched approach or just launch many small GEMM kernels. Launching 1024 kernels might be overhead, but we can use a single kernel that loops over the batch dimension for small GEMMs. We'll implement a specialized kernel for the attention scores and output computations, which processes one or more heads per block, using shared memory, and directly computes the attention output in one pass, avoiding separate GEMM launches and softmax kernel. This is a common fused attention kernel approach.\n\nGiven the time, I'll implement a fused attention kernel that processes one head per block, using shared memory to store Q, K, V for that head, computes scores, softmax, and output, all in one kernel. This reduces memory traffic and launches. We'll also need a separate kernel for the linear projections and final output projection. We can implement those as custom GEMM kernels with bias.\n\nLet's design the fused attention kernel:\n- Launch grid: batch_size * num_heads blocks (or multiple blocks per head if seq_len large; but seq_len=64, so one block per head).\n- Block size: seq_len * head_dim? Actually, we need enough threads to compute the output. We can have blockDim.x = head_dim (64), blockDim.y = seq_len (64), giving 4096 threads per block, which is too many for typical GPUs (max 1024 threads). We need to tile. Since seq_len=64, head_dim=64, the output size per head is 64x64 = 4096 elements. We can use a 2D block of 16x16 threads, each thread computes one output element or multiple. A 16x16 block (256 threads) is good. We'll need shared memory to store Q, K, V: 3 * 64*64 floats = 12288 floats = 48KB. That fits in shared memory (48KB per block on many GPUs). Plus we might need space for scores (16KB) but we can compute scores on the fly without storing the whole matrix, or we can store scores in registers or re-compute for softmax. Since softmax needs row-wise max and sum, we'll need to iterate over the key dimension twice: once for max, once for sum and output. That can be done using registers for each row's max and sum, and not store the full scores matrix. So shared memory only for Q, K, V (48KB). That's exactly 48KB, which is the default shared memory per block on many GPUs (like 48KB on V100, 48KB on A100? Actually A100 has up to 164KB per block, but default is 48KB). We can request dynamic shared memory of 48KB, which should be available. However, if the GPU has only 48KB per block, using all of it might limit occupancy, but we only need one block per SM, which is fine. So we can allocate 48KB dynamic shared memory.\n\nIn the kernel, we load Q, K, V for the given head into shared memory. Then each thread block of 16x16 threads (covering output positions) computes attention. Since seq_len=64, we can have blockDim=16x16, gridDim = (ceil(seq_len/16), ceil(seq_len/16), batch_size*num_heads). That's 4x4=16 blocks per head, but each block would need the entire Q,K,V for that head, so we would duplicate loads. Instead, we want one block per head to load Q,K,V once. But we can't have 64x64 threads. We can have a block of 256 threads arranged as 16x16, and each thread iterates over multiple output positions? That would be 4x4 iterations, but we need to compute softmax over rows, which requires each row to be processed by one thread (or group of threads). A common approach is to have each thread handle one query position (row) and loop over keys. If we have 256 threads and seq_len=64, we can assign each thread to a query position and have multiple threads per query if needed. But 64 threads per block is too few. We could use 128 or 256 threads, with each thread handling one query position and using vectorized loads. Since head_dim=64, each query has 64-dimensional Q, and keys are 64-dimensional. A thread can compute dot products sequentially. That might be fine for performance.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the standard attention kernel where each thread computes one output element, and softmax is computed using shared memory reduction across the row. That would require storing scores and doing a reduction per row. With 64 columns, a warp can handle one or two rows easily.\n\nGiven the moderate size, I'll implement a relatively simple fused attention kernel:\n- One block per head, block size = 256 threads.\n- Shared memory: Q, K, V arrays of size seq_len * head_dim each.\n- Load Q, K, V from global memory in a coalesced manner.\n- Each thread block handles one head; within the block, we compute output for all query positions. We'll assign each thread to compute one output element (or multiple). Since output size is seq_len * head_dim = 4096, we cannot map one thread per element with only 256 threads. So each thread will compute 16 elements (4096/256). We can organize as each thread computes a vertical strip: thread idx t (0-255) computes output rows t/4? Not efficient.\n\nMaybe simpler: use a 2D block of 16x16 threads and have each thread compute a 4x4 tile of the output (since 16*4=64). That works: block dims (16,16) -> 256 threads, grid dims (4,4, batch*heads) so that each block computes a 16x16 output tile by sliding over the Q and K dimensions. But each block would need the full Q and K for the corresponding head, so we'd load Q and K into shared memory per block, which is redundant across the 16 blocks for the same head. We could instead have one block per head and compute all 64x64 output, but with 256 threads, each thread computes multiple elements.\nI'll go with the 16x16 block approach: grid per head is (4,4). Each block loads the entire Q and K for that head into shared memory (64x64 each = 16KB each, total 32KB) plus V (16KB) = 48KB. That's 48KB per block, so each SM can only run one block. But we have 4x4=16 blocks per head, each doing redundant loads, which is inefficient. However, the total data per head is 48KB, and loading it 16 times per head means 768KB global reads per head, while the computation is 64*64*64*2 FLOPs = 524k FLOPs, so arithmetic intensity is low. But the entire model has 1024 heads, so total global loads would be very high. This approach would be memory-bound and likely slower than optimized libraries.\n\nA better approach is to have one block per head, loading Q,K,V once, and then compute output using a loop over tiles within the block. We can use a 2D thread block of 16x16 (256 threads), and each thread computes one output element for each iteration of a 4x4 grid over the output matrix. That is, we loop over tile positions, and within each tile, threads compute elements. Shared memory holds Q,K,V for the head. The block size 256, we have 16x16 threads. For each output tile (16x16), we compute that tile. But we need the entire Q and K rows/columns for that tile. Since Q and K are in shared memory, that's fine. We can compute the tile by iterating over the key dimension. This is essentially a tiled matrix multiplication with Q (64x64) and K^T (64x64), then softmax, then multiply by V. We can fuse these steps. This kernel will be efficient because Q,K,V are loaded once per head from global memory.\n\nLet's design the kernel:\n- Grid: batch_size * num_heads blocks.\n- Block: 16x16 threads (256 threads).\n- Shared memory: q_s[seq_len][head_dim], k_s[seq_len][head_dim], v_s[seq_len][head_dim]. (We'll use 64x64 each, so 3*4096 floats = 12288 floats = 48KB).\n- Also need to store scaling factor and mask.\n- Input Q, K, V pointers for this batch and head. Since our Q, K, V are stored in global memory as (batch, num_heads, seq_len, head_dim) after projection (we'll arrange that). So for block index = b * num_heads + h, we can compute the base pointer.\n\nInside the kernel:\n1. Load Q, K, V into shared memory cooperatively. Use all 256 threads to load 3*4096 elements = 12288 loads, which is 48 loads per thread. We can do a loop.\n2. __syncthreads();\n3. For each query tile (we'll process 4x4 tiles of 16x16): For each tile, each thread computes one element of the attention output for that tile. But we need to compute softmax for each query row, which involves the entire key dimension. Since we process tiles of rows, each tile handles 16 query rows. For each of those 16 rows, we need to compute max and sum across all keys (64). We can have each thread compute a partial max by iterating over its assigned keys, then use shared memory reduction across the 16 threads in the same row. We'll structure the computation as: For a tile of query rows (say rows 0-15), we compute the attention scores for each key for those rows, then softmax, then multiply by V. We'll need to compute scores on the fly, but we can load K from shared memory. We'll do this in two passes: first pass to compute max, second pass to compute sum and output. This is standard.\n\nBecause the tile is 16x16 (16 query rows, 16 output dims), and each thread (tx, ty) in the 16x16 block corresponds to a query row (ty) and output dim (tx), for a given key column, we can compute the dot product between Q[query_row, :] and K[key, :]. However, each thread only computes one output element, but the dot product depends on the query row, not the output dim. So we need to either compute the full score once per query row (shared among threads with same ty) or compute redundantly. A better mapping: each thread handles a query row, and we use a block of 64 threads per row? But we only have 256 threads. We can assign multiple query rows per block and use all threads cooperatively.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll simplify: I'll implement separate kernels for the attention scores with softmax and the multiplication with V, using cuBLAS for the GEMMs and a custom softmax kernel. Since the per-head GEMMs are batched, we can use cuBLAS strided batched GEMM, which is efficient. This will be simpler and likely performant enough.\n\nSo the plan:\n- Use cuBLAS for all GEMMs (projections, scores, attention output, out_proj).\n- Use custom kernels for bias addition, softmax with mask, and permutation.\n- Handle transposes correctly using cuBLAS parameters.\n\nAfter careful thought, I'll write the .cu file with the following functions:\n1. multiply_kernel and launcher (already written above).\n2. stack_kernel and launcher (already written above).\n3. add_bias_kernel and launcher for bias addition in projections.\n4. softmax_mask_kernel (already written above).\n5. permute_attn_kernel (already written above).\n6. multihead_attention_forward_launcher: the main function that orchestrates everything using cuBLAS and custom kernels.\n\nSince I cannot test, I need to be careful with the cuBLAS calls and memory layouts.\n\nI'll adopt the following convention for matrices:\n- All matrices are stored row-major.\n- For a GEMM C = A * B, where A is MxK, B is KxN, C is MxN, I will use cuBLAS with:\n  cublasSgemm(handle, CUBLAS_OP_N, CUBLAS_OP_N, N, M, K, &alpha, B, N, A, K, &beta, C, N);\n  Wait, this formula is from a known trick: To compute C_row = A_row * B_row, use:\n  cublasSgemm(handle, CUBLAS_OP_N, CUBLAS_OP_N, N, M, K, &alpha, B, N, A, K, &beta, C, N);\n  This assumes A and B are row-major but passed as column-major with swapped dimensions. Let's verify with a small example: M=2, K=3, N=2. A_row (2x3), B_row (3x2).\n  According to the trick, we pass B as the first matrix (N x K) but we pass B_row with leading dimension N=2, and A as the second matrix (K x M) with leading dimension K=3. The output C is N x M = 2x2, but we pass C with leading dimension N=2, and the result will be C_row. This is a well-known method to compute row-major matrices using cuBLAS column-major. I'll trust this trick.\n  So for C = A * B, I'll call:\n  cublasSgemm(handle, CUBLAS_OP_N, CUBLAS_OP_N, N, M, K, &alpha, B, N, A, K, &beta, C, N);\n  This works if A is stored in row-major with leading dimension K, B with leading dimension N, and C with leading dimension N.\n\nFor projections:\n  Q = query * Wq^T. Here A = query (MxK, with K=embed_dim), B = Wq (embed_dim x embed_dim), so B^T is also embed_dim x embed_dim. We want C = query * Wq^T, which is M x embed_dim. So in our GEMM, we need A_row = query, B_row = Wq^T (which is just Wq as row-major if Wq is symmetric? Wq is not symmetric, but Wq^T as row-major is the transpose of Wq. We have Wq stored as row-major (embed_dim x embed_dim). We need to pass B as Wq^T, but we can instead compute C = query * Wq^T as C^T = Wq * query^T. So we could compute C^T using the trick, then transpose C. But maybe we can directly compute C = query * Wq^T by noting that Wq^T is the same as Wq with swapped dimensions; if we treat Wq as column-major, we can use CUBLAS_OP_T on B.\n\nSince the trick for row-major GEMM uses both matrices with CUBLAS_OP_N, we could store Wq^T in a temporary buffer or use a different approach. To avoid transposing Wq, we can compute Q^T = Wq * query^T, and then transpose Q^T to get Q. Computing Q^T = Wq * query^T is a GEMM where A = Wq (KxK), B = query^T (KxM). Using the trick, we can call C^T = B * A, but that would compute query * Wq, not Wq * query^T. Hmm.\n\nActually, the trick works for any row-major A and B. To compute C = A * B, we call cublasSgemm with A=B, B=A, m=N, n=M, k=K. So if we want C = Wq * query^T (KxM), we set A=Wq, B=query^T, so we need B as query^T (KxM) row-major. But query^T is the transpose of query, which we don't have unless we transpose query. So we would still need a transpose.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think it's simplest to write our own GEMM kernels for all operations. This avoids cuBLAS transposes and allows easy bias addition. We'll implement a general GEMM kernel with tiling and shared memory, and use it for all large GEMMs. The per-head small GEMMs can also use this kernel by launching many blocks, but we can optimize with a batched version or a fused attention kernel. To keep things manageable, I'll implement a tiled GEMM kernel and launch it for each GEMM call. For the attention scores and output, we can use the same GEMM kernel with appropriate parameters. The number of launches will be high for the batched small GEMMs (2 * 1024 = 2048 launches), which is too many. So we need a specialized kernel for the sequence of batched GEMMs, or use a single kernel that loops over batches. I'll implement a batched GEMM kernel that takes arrays of pointers and sizes, and processes all batches in one kernel launch. That will be efficient.\n\nLet's implement a batched GEMM kernel for small matrices with constant dimensions across the batch. Specifically, for attention scores: each batch item is a GEMM of (seq_len x seq_len) = (seq_len x head_dim) * (head_dim x seq_len). We can have a kernel where each block handles one GEMM (one batch/head pair). We'll use 16x16 thread blocks and compute the entire 64x64 output in one block by looping over tiles. This is exactly the same as a standard tiled GEMM kernel but with batch dimension mapped to blockIdx.z. So we can write a kernel with grid dims (ceil(seq_len/16), ceil(seq_len/16), batch_count), block dims (16,16). Shared memory holds tiles of A and B. This is a standard tiled GEMM.\n\nSimilarly, for attention output: each GEMM is (seq_len x head_dim) = (seq_len x seq_len) * (seq_len x head_dim). Same kernel can be used with appropriate parameters.\n\nFor the large projection GEMMs, we can use a similar tiled GEMM kernel with larger grids. Since the dimensions are large (M=8192, N=512, K=512), we can use a 2D grid and block, with each block computing a 32x32 tile. This is fine.\n\nThus, I'll write a general tiled GEMM kernel:\ntemplate<int BLOCK_M, int BLOCK_N, int BLOCK_K>\n__global__ void gemm_kernel(const float* A, const float* B, float* C,\n                            int M, in